  "metadata": {
    "title": "Eigenvectors: directions a matrix only stretches",
    "subject": "Mathematics",
    "topic": "Eigenvectors",
    "objective": "Show a vector that keeps its direction under a linear transformation while its length scales.",
    "learner": "Knows matrix multiplication.",
    "misconceptions": [
      "Every vector is an eigenvector, since every vector is mapped somewhere",
      "An eigenvector has to be a unit vector"
    ],
    "expectedUnderstanding": [
      "A is the map (x, y) to (2x + y, x + 2y); Av is drawn beside v and both start at O.",
      "The direction of v survives only for slopes 1 and -1, that is directions (1, 1) and (1, -1); every other slope leaves Av off the line through v.",
      "Where the direction survives, Av is a multiple of v: 3v for (1, 1) and 1v for (1, -1), and that multiple is the eigenvalue.",
      "Any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is an eigenvector, so (5, 5) works as well as (1, 1); a general real matrix need not have any real eigenvector, a rotation for example."
    ]
  },
