  "narration": [
    {
      "at": 0.9,
      "text": "The matrix A has rows (2, 1) and (1, 2). It sends the blue vector v to the red vector Av. Both arrows start at the origin."
    },
    {
      "at": 3.4,
      "text": "The grey dashes are the line through v and O. If Av stays on that line, the direction of v survived the map."
    },
    {
      "at": 6.6,
      "text": "Here it does: Av points the same way as v and is three times as long. Such a vector is an eigenvector, and 3 is its eigenvalue."
    },
    {
      "at": 10.0,
      "text": "Move the slider away from that slope and the red arrow leaves the line at once. Most vectors are not eigenvectors at all."
    },
    {
      "at": 13.8,
      "text": "At slope -1 the arrows lie on top of each other: Av equals v, so the eigenvalue is 1. The direction survives; the length does not grow."
    },
    {
      "at": 17.0,
      "text": "The two dashed guides mark those directions, (1, 1) and (1, -1). Every non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is one too, (5, 5) as well as (1, 1). Other matrices, a rotation for instance, may have no real eigenvector at all."
    }
  ],
  "fallback": {
    "text": "Static figure. Axes with origin O, and the matrix map (x, y) to (2x + y, x + 2y). For five slopes m the vector v = (1, m) is drawn from O in blue and Av in red, with a grey dashed line through v and O. m = 1: Av = (3, 3), three times v, on the line. m = -1: Av = (1, -1) = v, on the line, the two arrows coincide. m = 0: Av = (2, 1), off the line. m = 0.5: Av = (2.5, 2), off the line. m = -1.4: Av = (0.6, -1.8), off the line. Two dashed guides mark the directions (1, 1) and (1, -1). Caption: only the eigen-directions are mapped onto their own line; on that line the map is multiplication by the eigenvalue, 3 or 1, and any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is again one."
  },
  "accessibility": {
    "alt": "A pair of axes with origin O. A blue arrow v runs from O to the point (1, m) and a red arrow Av runs from O to the point (2 + m, 1 + 2m), with a dashed grey line through O and v. As the slope slider sweeps, the blue arrow tilts and the red arrow follows. At slope 1 the red arrow lies on the dashed line, three times the blue one; at slope -1 the two arrows coincide; at almost every other slope the red arrow stands clearly off the dashed line. Two dashed guides, revealed at the end, mark the surviving directions (1, 1) and (1, -1).",
    "reducedMotion": "static",
    "keyboard": true
  },
